# kernel — real-world examples

## What kernel am I running, and what's installed?

```sh
uname -r
rpm -q kernel-default          # RPM systems (Tumbleweed: kernel-default)
```

## OpenSUSE Tumbleweed: kernels are managed automatically

```sh
# Tumbleweed keeps old kernels per /etc/zypp/zypp.conf:
#   multiversion.kernels = latest,latest-1,running
# Cleanup happens automatically via purge-kernels.service
systemctl status purge-kernels.service

# List installed kernels
rpm -qa 'kernel-default*' | sort

# Kernel updates arrive with the normal rolling upgrade
sudo zypper dup
```

## RHEL/CentOS style: remove old kernels manually

```sh
rpm -q kernel
sudo yum remove kernel-<old-version>    # recommend keeping 3 installed
```

## Debian/Ubuntu style: list and purge old kernels

```sh
dpkg --list | grep linux-image
dpkg --list | grep linux-headers

sudo apt-get purge linux-image-3.19.0-{18,20,21}
sudo apt-get purge linux-headers-3.19.0-{18,20,21}
sudo update-grub2

# Upgrade path
apt-cache search linux-kernel
apt-get install linux-image-<flavor>
```

## Homelab: confirm the running kernel after a zypper dup + reboot

```sh
ssh david@homelab 'uname -r && rpm -qa kernel-default | sort'
```

## Homelab: boot an older kernel once if the new one misbehaves

```sh
# Hold Esc/F2 at boot for the GRUB menu -> "Advanced options" -> pick the older kernel
# Then check what broke:
journalctl -k -b -p err
```

| Command                     | What it does                       |
| --------------------------- | ---------------------------------- |
| uname -r                    | running kernel version             |
| rpm -qa 'kernel-default*'   | installed kernels (SUSE)           |
| zypper dup                  | rolling upgrade incl. kernel       |
| journalctl -k -b            | kernel log for current boot        |
